 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSockets: the server holds a socket session per connected browser, the change store records every committed edit as a replayable revision, and delivery publishes each revision to connected sessions as it commits. A browser whose socket drops reconnects and catches up from the last revision it received.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+Change storage, delivery, and browser recovery are designed together around this mechanism: the change store keeps the revision history that replay reads, delivery owns socket capacity and publication reliability, and browser sync owns reconnect and catch-up.
 
